WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.100
Forty years after John Carpenter's classic
horror Halloween first hit the big screen,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.380
Jamie Lee Curtis is back as Laurie Strode
in a sequel that sees the former babysitter

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.880
attempt to turn the tables on the murderous
Michael Myers.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.000
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm revealing the secret truth about

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.340
the new Halloween movie and Michael Myers,
including 10 freaky facts you need to know

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.349
about the film!

00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:27.610
If you're new here, I do regular deep-dives
into your favourite flicks, so tap the bell

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.710
and also leave me a comment about the movie
for a chance to win one of these cool Halloween

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.150
merch packs.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.380
And make sure you stay to the end of the video
to find out some secret cameos in the film!

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.430
When it came to creating memorable death scenes
for the inhabitants of Haddonfield , the filmmakers

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.820
behind the new movie wanted to make the kind
of striking images that are still "burned

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.860
into [their] brains like terrifying postcards"
many years after they first saw John Carpenter's

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.420
original Halloween.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.050
But just how did the new creative team go
about producing spine-chilling images of Michael

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.940
Myers' victims after their deaths?

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.470
Well, once director David Gordon Green knew
exactly what deathly scene he wanted to be

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:09.110
seared into audiences' minds, Oscar-winning
Special Effects Make-up Designer Christopher

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:14.090
Nelson set about creating it by making life-casts
of the actors whose characters are killed

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.860
with specific expressions.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:21.100
The designer then re-sculpted the casts for
dramatic effect, slightly over-accentuating

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.530
their expressions and stylising them "to give
a terrifying inhumanity".

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:30.330
In fact, Nelson's creations turned out to
be so freaky that when actress Andi Matichak,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:35.070
who plays Jamie Lee Curtis's on-screen granddaughter,
first saw one of the corpses he'd made in

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:40.360
his workshop, it was so horrifically real
that she refused to go anywhere near it!

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.659
To bring The Shape, aka Michael Myers, back
to life, the filmmakers turned to actor-stuntman,

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:49.630
James Jude Courtney, who impressed them with
his ability to "channel [the character's]

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.360
enigmatic, methodical nature".

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.180
And Courtney found inspiration for his performance
in some rather freaky places.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.120
First of all, there was Courtney's encounter
with a real-life hitman who he met many years

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.439
earlier "through a mutual acquaintance".

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:07.030
The professional assassin lived with the actor
for a while as he "wanted his life story written".

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.909
And Courtney says he absorbed a lot "just
by hanging out with [the hitman] every day".

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:12.379
But that's not all.

00:02:12.379 --> 00:02:16.529
After watching a film about a contract killer
that Courtney appeared in, the real-life assassin

00:02:16.529 --> 00:02:20.400
gave the actor the low-down on how trained
killers really work.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.529
Years later, Courtney took what he learned
from that experience, especially the hitman's

00:02:24.529 --> 00:02:29.090
emphasis on the importance of stealth and
efficiency, and translated it into his portrayal

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.129
of Michael Myers and the efficient way he
kills his victims.

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:37.389
In fact, according to Jamie Lee Curtis, "something
changed when [Courtney] put [Myers'] mask

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:38.389
on."

00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:42.709
"The combination of [his] lack of human response
and his physical strength came out in a visceral

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:44.099
and emotional way."

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:49.180
For his version of Michael Myers, Courtney
didn't just channel contract killers though;

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.200
he also took cues from cats!

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.200
Yes, to help the actor create a Myers' that
was capable of emotionless rampage, director

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.709
David Gordon Green suggested he look at "cats
in the wild [such as] leopards and cheetahs

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:04.099
[…] that don't rationalise anything other
than what's in their path".

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:08.019
So, to get the kind of "cat-like quality to
[Myers'] […] movement" which the director

00:03:08.019 --> 00:03:12.909
wanted on screen, Courtney used the way his
own cat plays and hunts as a reference.

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:17.260
And this isn't the first time the actor has
looked to a cat for ideas about a character.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.849
In the late 90s, Courtney appeared in an episode
of Buffy the Vampire Slayer, and "used a cat

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:24.840
to design [his] movement" for his demonic
character on the show.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.040
For Courtney, "cats are the most perfect hunting,
killing machines on the planet."

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.310
"And the beauty of it is we don't judge a
cat for what a cat does."

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.920
So the actor "carried that movement and the
non-judgmental approach to the way [he] moved

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.249
as The Shape".

00:03:38.249 --> 00:03:42.609
Nick Castle, who played the original Michael
Myers in the very first Halloween movie, also

00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:46.959
passed on some valuable nuggets of information
to the filmmakers about playing The Shape

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.650
based on his own experience of the role in
the 70s.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.790
Castle gave advice about little subtle character
traits, for example, how Michael Myers turns

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:59.359
or tilts his head to observe a kill and also
how he sits up at a 90-degree angle without

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:03.249
using his hands, all of which the director
found useful for the new film.

00:04:03.249 --> 00:04:08.109
However, Nick Castle actually credits director
John Carpenter as the person who came up with

00:04:08.109 --> 00:04:12.459
the idea of the now classic head tilt back
in 1978.

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:16.600
According to Castle, during filming, Carpenter
told him to stand and look at the character

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.380
Myers had just killed, then tilt his head
to the right, then to the left.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:26.030
At the time, Castle didn't realise what Carpenter
was trying to achieve with that little instruction,

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:30.410
but when he saw the final film he was blown
away by the director's brilliant idea to make

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.150
it look like Myers was admiring his kill.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.280
Most of the time you see The Shape on screen,
he's being played by James Jude Courtney.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.650
However, fans of John Carpenter's classic
will be happy to hear that the original actor

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.680
Nick Castle does have a cameo in the new movie.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.750
Make sure you look out for the first time
that Laurie sees The Shape because that's

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.330
Castle under the Michael Myers mask.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:56.620
Director David Gordon Green specifically asked
the veteran actor to perform Myers' classic

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.250
head tilt once more, a move which apparently
gave everyone on set goosebumps!

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:05.770
And actually, Castle also got to pass the
Michael Myers torch on to new actor James

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.860
Jude Courtney in that same scene.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.070
"When Laurie sees the Shape, that's Nick and
I in the same scene."

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.540
"So he's in the first shot in the reveal."

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.060
"And I'm the second part of that shot."

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.980
"And then I carry the torch from that point
forward."

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.390
"So it's literally handing the baton, man,
it was beautiful."

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:20.390
"Yeah."

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:24.280
Now, while Castle's on-screen time as Myers
is short, that's not his only contribution

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.280
to the new movie.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.950
Listen carefully for any scenes where you
can hear Myers breathing because the sounds

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:35.460
you hear are actually Nick Castle who recorded
The Shape's breathing in post-production!

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.610
An absolutely crucial part of the Halloween
movies is Myers' creepy mask, which actually

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.610
started out life as a store-bought William
Shatner Captain Kirk mask before its eye-holes

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:49.950
were made bigger, its hair was altered, and
it was spray-painted white, all of which gave

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.660
it the iconic look we know today.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:56.080
For the new movie, rather than put their own
spin on the mask, the filmmakers decided to

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.480
try to recreate the original's strange, soulless
mannequin-like face and, most importantly,

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:03.380
the feeling it inspired in audiences.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:08.450
But it wasn't nearly as simple as it sounds
because four decades have passed since Michael

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:13.110
Myers first wore the mask and the latex would
have changed shape after so long in evidence

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.110
storage.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:17.860
Fortunately, the movie had Special Effects
Make-up Designer Christopher Nelson on board

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:19.700
to get the right look.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.800
Nelson has a real grasp of the history of
both the franchise and the mask as he's been

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.800
a fan of the series since he saw the first
movie when he 10 years old.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.900
The designer researched how 40-year-old latex
decomposes and studied how it looks when it

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.370
gets brittle, wrinkles, warps, and sags, all
of which was essential for how he created

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.480
the new mask while still keeping the expression
and form of the original one.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:45.272
For Nelson, another vital element of the mask
from the original film was that it looked

00:06:45.272 --> 00:06:49.880
different in each shot; so he channelled that
feeling into the new film making the new,

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:54.750
deteriorated mask look different at every
angle, to give a sense that the mask was constantly

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:56.760
shape-shifting.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.140
According to Jamie Lee Curtis, her "[unofficial]
godson" Jake Gyllenhaal was so keen for her

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:05.400
to work with director David Gordon Green that
he "tracked her down while she was on vacation

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.610
in the mountains".

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:10.501
Gyllenhaal was really enthusiastic about the
amazing creative experience he'd recently

00:07:10.501 --> 00:07:14.050
had working with Green on the Boston marathon
bombing film, Stronger.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:17.890
And that convinced Curtis to speak with the
director about his ideas for a new Halloween

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:18.890
movie.

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:22.240
However, when she signed on to return to the
screen once more as Laurie Strode, Curtis

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.880
assumed the movie would focus more on Laurie's
daughter and granddaughter.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:30.600
And it was only once she started working through
her character's psychological trauma in rehearsals

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.930
that she realised the real depth of the work
she'd taken on.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:38.450
Not only was the shoot emotionally moving
with plenty of tears, but it was also physically

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:43.139
draining in terms of stunt work, and Curtis
ended up with a cracked rib after shooting

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:44.610
the film's final sequences.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:49.290
In fact, she says "it took a good month [to
recover]" afterwards.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:53.150
Given the story's late-October setting and
several memorable moments in the original

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:57.010
movie including its opening credits, it's
hardly surprising to see jack-o'-lanterns

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:58.600
on display in the new film.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:02.740
But getting hold of the hundreds of fresh
pumpkins that were needed in time for the

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:07.450
movie's mid-January shoot in Charleston, South
Carolina, proved to be rather tricky.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:12.210
Set decorator Missy Ricker ended up having
to bring in truck-loads of them from North

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:16.760
Carolina, and she also had to ensure she had
many dozens of pumpkins of the same size and

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:21.560
shape so they could be used for any scenes
that needed to be filmed many times over.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:26.230
On top of that, the set-decorating team also
had to work out how to preserve the pumpkins

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:28.840
so they were always camera-ready throughout
the shoot.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.510
The key to that particular problem turned
out to be dipping them in vats of a bleach

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:33.580
solution!

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:38.909
Nick Castle isn't the only star of the original
film who makes a brief return in the new movie.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:42.950
Actor Donald Pleasence's character Dr Samuel
Loomis has been a major part of the Halloween

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:48.230
franchise since it began, and while Pleasence
passed away in the 1990s, Dr Loomis still

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.750
gets a vocal cameo in the new film.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.839
Although it's not the voice of Pleasence himself,
the filmmakers are pretty pleased with the

00:08:54.839 --> 00:08:58.230
result of the sound-alike voice actor they
found for the role.

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:02.249
Of course, this isn't the first time that
Dr Loomis has been recreated by a voice actor

00:09:02.249 --> 00:09:04.740
as that also happened in the 1998 film, Halloween:
H20.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:10.220
And, I'm going to talk more about Loomis in
my Halloween Deleted scenes video, which you

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:11.769
can tap here to watch.

00:09:11.769 --> 00:09:15.129
By the way, in this new film, listen out for
P.J.

00:09:15.129 --> 00:09:19.680
Soles who played Laurie's friend Lynda in
the 1978 movie, and who makes a brief appearance

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.110
[here] as a teacher.

